# The Clockmaker's Last Apprentice

Wind the mainspring, lad, as I have told;
My hands are spent; the work's grown cold.
The bench must pass to thee, my boy;
Take up the tools I've used and enjoy.

Tick by tick, the wheels go round;
I've kept them working on this ground.
But some springs break beyond repair;
We keep them close, and that's quite fair.

Rust claims iron, slow and sure;
No oil stops the creeping blur.
We cannot mend what age has worn;
We guard what's left; that's all we yearn.

Chime the bell when work is done;
Leave the bench for me, my son.
The old clocks stop, but thou dost keep
Their ticking memory while you sleep.

Stop and think, my lad: what can we save
When even brass will fade?
We hold what breaks; that's all thou'dst crave -
Can keeping close, then, be our aid?
